  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 333 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nCurrently, a mid/upper level short wave trough is sweeping across \nthe central part of the country while weak ridging is being \ndisplaced eastward. Low clouds remain across a sliver of central \nKansas as well as far eastern Kansas associated with shallow \nmoisture under the ridge. As the next wave passes across the region, \nadditional clouds will shift into the region tonight through \nSaturday morning. Patchy fog may also be a concern again across \ncentral Kansas late tonight/early Saturday morning. Overnight lows \nwill fall into the 30s for most, though the magnitude of the cold \nair may not be realized due to the low/mid level clouds. Patchy \nfrost remains possible for much of the area. Clouds will gradually \nclear from west to east as the short wave moves across the region \nand temperatures will begin to rebound. High on Saturday will range \nfrom the lower 60s across central Kansas to the middle 50s in \nsoutheast Kansas.\n\nFor Sunday into mid-week, broad ridging will build over the Western \nUS and across the Plains. Dry weather, mostly clear conditions, and \nabove normal temperatures are forecast through Wednesday of next \nweek. Models are hinting at another shortwave towards middle/end of \nnext week as a deep trough digs into the Northeast US. At this \npoint, this feature looks to have minimal ramifications across the \nforecast area and may only lead to a slight cool-down for the end of \nnext week.",
